        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nThe logs say something about thread starvation. What is that telling me?",
          "label": "evals/akka-diagnostics/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "00a83e7e1b4aa396265c545e5a1e7a7b582a4bc074e8af9734ee6017ba71f2de",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "The Akka Thread Starvation Detector is telling you a dispatcher's threads were so busy that a trivial probe task waited longer than the threshold (default 100ms) to run. Meanwhile actor mailboxes, `Future` callbacks, and cluster heartbeats on that dispatcher sit waiting too.\n\n**Usual cause:** blocking work (JDBC, blocking HTTP, `Await`, `Thread.sleep`, file IO) running on the default dispatcher. It shows up as high latency or timeouts with low CPU.\n\n**What to do:**\n1. Read the stack traces in the warning. They aggregate what the busy threads were doing, and the repeated frames point to the blocking call. Native blocking IO can show as `RUNNABLE`, so trust the traces over thread states.\n2. Move that call onto a dedicated `thread-pool-executor` dispatcher, as a bulkhead. See `akka-actors` dispatchers.\n3. Don't raise the threshold or enlarge the default dispatcher to make the warning go away. That hides the problem.\n\n**If it keeps happening:** starvation can also cause false unreachable members or remoting quarantine. If you see those in the same logs, fix the blocking first and don't shorten `acceptable-heartbeat-pause`.\n\nPaste the warning and its stack traces if you want me to point at the offending call. I haven't seen your logs yet."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
